  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1039 PM MDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nA large precipitation shield has been lifting northward through\nthe CWA this evening associated with a mid level circulation \nthe models have handled fairly well the past few days. The last \npiece to lift through will occur on Friday as an strong upstream\nsystem arriving to the PacNW coast kicks a trough out of the\nDesert Southwest and across the Rockies. There will be a battle\nbetween clouds and sun on Friday but expect a transition to  \nconvective mode by the afternoon behind lingering morning \nstratiform rainfall. Instability remains moderate along and if\nall the CAPE can be realized and then released, some stronger\nstorms can be expected. Heavy rainfall...small hail and gusty\nout flow winds will all be threats. Flat ridging moves in\novernight into Saturday leaving a much drier and warmer forecast\nin place to start the weekend. The remainder of the forecast\ngets a bit more interesting and thus challenging. A stronger\nupstream trough arrives on the Pacific coast Sunday \nafternoon...pushing the ridge east of the Rockies and backing \nthe upper flow to the southwest over the Great Basin. Moisture \nfrom a pair of tropical systems...including the weakening \nPolo...will be drawn into the southwest flow aloft ahead of the\ntrough. PWATs will again return to 150-200 percent of normal \nover our area by early Monday and bring the start of another wet\nperiod through at least midweek. If this trough slows, any moisture\ncould be increased and the duration longer into the week. The \nGFS has been been consistently the more progressive and thus \ndrier solution. Ensembles and even the cluster solutions have \nbeen inconsistent on how to handle the moisture and upstream \ntrough so confidence remains low on a final solution attm. There\nwill be an uptick in precipitation from Sunday afternoon into \nearly next week. Whether this will be a significant/anomalous \nevent for the 4 Corners region is yet to be determined. Stay \ntuned for later forecasts as this develops.",
